<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Minimal Arcade version file for the 16.11 servicing line.

     16.11 does NOT control product or dependency versions from here. Package versions live in
     scripts/build/TestPlatform.Dependencies.props and the product version in
     scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets, exactly as they always have. This file exists
     only because the Arcade SDK requires it. Do not grow it into a second source of truth. -->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>16.11.1</VersionPrefix>
    <PreReleaseVersionLabel>release</PreReleaseVersionLabel>
    <PreReleaseVersionIteration></PreReleaseVersionIteration>
    <!-- AssemblyVersion is pinned at 15.0.0 because the wire protocol serializes against it. -->
    <AssemblyVersion>15.0.0</AssemblyVersion>

    <!-- Arcade would otherwise add implicit test-package references. 16.11 pins its own MSTest,
         xunit and NUnit versions in scripts/build/TestPlatform.Dependencies.props, and a second
         implicit reference is NETSDK1023 on every test project. -->
    <!-- Arcade resolves its test plumbing from TestRunnerName: RunTests is defined in
         tools\$(TestRunnerName)\$(TestRunnerName).targets. UsingToolXUnit=false BLANKS that name
         unconditionally — Tests.targets:16 has no guard on the current value — so the runner
         targets are never imported and every test project fails with
           MSB4057: The target "RunTests" does not exist in the project.
         MSTest is the name that works: there is no tools\VSTest\ directory, VSTest.targets sits at
         the tools root, and MSTest.targets:29 imports it when EnableMSTestRunner is false, which is
         already its default. rel/17.14 sets this for the same reason.
         These two properties must move together or the second silently undoes the first. -->
    <TestRunnerName>MSTest</TestRunnerName>
    <!-- 16.11 localizes through src/package/Localize/Localize.proj with fmdev.xlftool. Arcade's
         XliffTasks would be a second, conflicting mechanism. -->
    <!-- Do not set UsingToolXliff=true. Tried and measured: modern XliffTasks cannot read this line's
         .xlf files at all -

           Resources.cs.xlf : error : The element 'trans-unit' has invalid child element 'target'.
                              Expected: context-group, count-group, prop-group, note, alt-trans

         a schema difference between the 2021 .xlf files and current XliffTasks, so enabling the tool
         fails the build outright rather than degrading output.

         This is a second and stronger reason for hazard 1 than the one originally recorded. Hazard 1
         said the tool must stay off because it deletes non-translatable units and breaks satellite
         byte alignment; in fact it cannot parse these files at all.

         The consequence is that this line produces NO satellite assemblies from the Arcade build,
         because Arcade Localization.targets:9 gates satellite generation on the same switch. That is
         a real gap - the shipped artifact has 13 locales - and it must be solved another way, most
         likely through src\package\Localize\Localize.proj which is this line's own localization
         path. UpdateXlfOnBuild is not the answer: it is a sub-setting of a block that never runs.

         Zero .xlf files were modified by the experiment. That is a STATE observation - the tree
         returned unchanged - and it was originally written here as "the negative control held",
         which borrows a stronger word than the check earns. A negative control would establish that
         the check can detect a modification when one occurs; this establishes only that none
         occurred, which is the evidence that the block never runs, not evidence that the check
         works. Naming corrected rather than machinery added: the observation is sound and its
         label was not. -->
    <UsingToolXliff>false</UsingToolXliff>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
